\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[review]{acl}
\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{xcolor}
\usepackage{subcaption}

\definecolor{speccolor}{HTML}{C44E52}
\definecolor{lingcolor}{HTML}{4C72B0}

\title{Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal: Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\input{sections/abstract}
\input{sections/introduction}
\input{sections/related_work}
\input{sections/taxonomy}
\input{sections/method}
\input{sections/experiments}
\input{sections/analysis}
\input{sections/discussion}
\input{sections/conclusion}
\input{sections/limitations}

\bibliography{references}

\end{document}

\section{Error Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

To understand \emph{why} different ambiguity types produce different violation rates, we analyze 245 violation traces across GPT-5.4 (123) and GPT-4.1 (122), coded into five failure modes: unauthorized escalation, assumption-based action, scope misapplication, arbitrary rule selection, and conservative refusal. Inter-coder reliability for the failure mode taxonomy is substantial (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.782$); Cohen's $\kappa = 0.594$ is lower due to the prevalence paradox (the dominant category, \emph{assumption-based action}, accounts for 33\% of coded instances, inflating chance agreement and depressing $\kappa$).

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Signatures}

Each ambiguity type produces a distinctive failure profile. Authorization scope violations are dominated by unauthorized escalation (72\% for GPT-5.4, 83\% for GPT-4.1): agents exceed permission boundaries when the scope of ``authorized personnel'' or ``approved actions'' is underspecified. Incompleteness triggers assumption-based action (61\% and 66\%): agents fill information gaps by inventing plausible but incorrect defaults. Scopal ambiguity leads to scope misapplication (76\% and 74\%): agents attach quantifiers, negation, or modals to the wrong constituent. Lexical ambiguity also triggers assumption-based action (71\% and 53\%), as agents select one word sense without considering alternatives. Conditional precedence produces a flatter distribution, split between scope misapplication (41\% and 28\%) and arbitrary rule selection (29\% and 28\%), reflecting the multiple resolution strategies available when explicit rules conflict.

These signatures are consistent across the two annotated models: five of six types share the same dominant failure mode in both GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1. Only coreferential diverges, with GPT-5.4 favoring scope misapplication (44\%) and GPT-4.1 favoring assumption-based action (38\%). The overall failure mode distribution is nearly identical between models ($\sim$33\% assumption-based action, $\sim$29\% unauthorized escalation, $\sim$27\% scope misapplication).

\subsection{Layer Mechanism}

Based on the two annotated models, the failure mode taxonomy reveals distinct layer-level mechanisms. Linguistic-layer types are dominated by scope misapplication (39\% average vs.\ 15\% for specification-layer), representing a ``misparse-and-apply'' pattern: the agent constructs an incorrect parse of the ambiguous clause and follows it consistently. This parallels human garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982}: agents commit to the first available parse without revision, consistent with the good-enough processing framework \citep{ferreira_good_enough_2002}. Specification-layer types are dominated by unauthorized escalation and assumption-based action ($\sim$65\% combined), representing a ``guess-and-act'' pattern: the agent recognizes that information is needed but proceeds with a fabricated resolution. The guess-and-act mechanism produces higher violation rates because the agent actively extends its authority, whereas misparse-and-apply errors are bounded by the clause's literal content. This mechanistic distinction is consistent with the directional trend in the aggregate layer comparison (C3, $\Delta = 6.08$ pp), though the aggregate rate difference remains inconclusive under within-judge analysis (\S\ref{sec:results}).

\subsection{Ambiguity Blindness}

Agents rarely recognize that a clause is ambiguous. We operationalize \emph{disambiguation awareness} as an episode in which the agent explicitly acknowledges multiple interpretations, asks a clarifying question, or conditions its action on the ambiguity; \emph{surface adoption} is coded when the agent proceeds with a single interpretation without acknowledgment. Across all types, GPT-5.4 shows 4.1\% disambiguation awareness and GPT-4.1 shows 10.7\%. Surface adoption exceeds 88\% for every type in both models, and escalation to the user occurs in fewer than 1\% of episodes. The 2.6$\times$ awareness gap suggests frontier models may be \emph{more} action-oriented. We term this phenomenon \emph{Ambiguity Blindness}: agents process ambiguous clauses without meta-cognitive awareness, adopting surface interpretations by default.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

Among the 300 ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3 of 5 models violate), with 13\% producing universal violations (all 5 models). This convergent-but-wrong pattern extends Berry and Kamsties' binary distinction between nocuous (divergent interpretations) and innocuous (convergent) ambiguity \citep{berry_kamsties_2004}: a third category, \emph{systematic nocuity}, arises when all models converge on the same incorrect interpretation. Systematic nocuity is more dangerous than divergent nocuity because it is invisible in inter-model agreement metrics and would appear ``correct'' without ground-truth comparison. Incompleteness shows the highest universal convergence rate (24\% of clauses trigger all 5 models), consistent with its status as a universal vulnerability.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on the original release of $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch).

Clause pairs are generated through a template-based pipeline and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background). Each annotator independently verified the three quality criteria; rejected or modified pairs were iteratively revised until both annotators confirmed compliance. For each type, we identify policy clauses in $\tau^2$-bench that are naturally susceptible to the target ambiguity, then construct the ambiguous and unambiguous variants. All pairs are reviewed to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target ambiguity type, (2) the unambiguous variant eliminates that ambiguity without introducing new information that changes the policy's scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural (would occur in real policy documents). A post-hoc realism validation confirms that ambiguous clauses are linguistically consistent with original $\tau^2$-bench policy text (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models spanning three providers and two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, Qwen3, and DeepSeek traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 traces. We acknowledge this creates an asymmetry (a mid-tier model judging a frontier model in one direction), which we discuss in \S\ref{sec:limitations}. Judges classify each episode outcome on a four-tier severity scale: none, minor, moderate, and critical. Our primary analysis uses moderate-or-above severity as the violation threshold; minor-only deviations (6.6\% of episodes) are excluded. Including minor violations (overall rate 49.6\% vs.\ 41.0\%) produces even larger effects without changing any directional conclusion.

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm that the observed effect is specific to semantic ambiguity rather than general prompt sensitivity, we run 120 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations: synonym substitution (replacing content words with near-synonyms) and clause reordering (shuffling the order of policy sub-clauses). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = 3.33$ pp (95\% CI $[-5.0, 13.3]$, containing zero), compared to the ambiguity effect of $\Delta_{\text{ambig}} = 34.0$ pp. A post-hoc sensitivity analysis indicates that with $n = 120$ episodes at $\alpha = 0.05$ and 80\% power, the design can detect perturbation effects $\geq 15$ pp, sufficient to rule out effects approaching the ambiguity magnitude but not small perturbation effects.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} To verify that violation rates are stable across independent runs, we replicate 30 clause pairs (selected via stratified random sampling, 5 per type) $\times$ 2 models $\times$ 3 replicates. The intraclass correlation coefficient is ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$), indicating excellent reproducibility.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes, $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus type and model effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, TOST equivalence testing \citep{lakens_2017}, and bootstrap 95\% CIs (10{,}000 resamples). TOST uses $\pm 15$ pp as the primary margin (one additional violation per seven episodes); we provide sensitivity analyses across $\pm 8$--$20$ pp. Holm--Bonferroni correction is applied within three families: (i) six per-type Fisher tests, (ii) five per-model McNemar tests, and (iii) fifteen pairwise type comparisons. C3's individual $p = 0.017$ does not survive correction within the four-claim family, consistent with its classification as inconclusive.

